  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is $T=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ Indeed, consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067709/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we can find the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{\u2032} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{\u2032} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067709/assets/0e37a258ea833a2d490fc34a34af40a5c7a18f7589f107c6d0ed631b331c8f2a.png)",
